  "slug": "valley-magnetic-0172",
  "summary": "Recent preprints show optical, electrical and magnetic switching of valley and exciton states in van der Waals stacks.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is using an ordered degree of freedom as an external handle on excitonic and valley states in van der Waals materials. In rhombohedrally stacked bilayer WSe2, sliding ferroelectricity gives electrically switchable, hysteretic exciton photoluminescence and Raman response, and in few-layer R-stacked WSe2 it switches dipolar exciton transport and drives dipole reconfiguration toward quadrupolar excitons. In WS2/CrSBr heterostructures, type-II band alignment, magnetic-proximity Zeeman valley splitting and spin-selective interlayer charge transfer couple valley and magnetic excitons. In WSe2/CrPS4, a light-controlled Néel vector switches valley polarization optically via spin-polarized charge transfer. The NbOCl2 preprint reports room-temperature antiferroelectricity with reversible switching, probed by SHG, but does not address valley or exciton states directly. What is new is the breadth of switchable order parameters, magnetic, ferroelectric and optical, used to control valley and exciton states.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-22"
}
